              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "subdūcō, subdūcere — \"to haul ashore, to draw up (a ship); to take away\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Hieme nautae nāvēs in lītus subdūcunt. In winter the sailors haul the ships up on the shore."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "subdūcō, subdūcere, subdūxī, subductum. Ancient ships were pulled up on the beach for the winter."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "subdūcō is built from sub- 'from under, up' and dūcō 'to lead, to draw' (EtymDB; Lewis & Short). English subduction comes from the noun subductiō."
            }
          ]
        },
